WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.080
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:10.780
Approximately a year ago, we talked about
an absolutely amazing paper by the name vid2game,

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.030
in which we could grab a controller and become
video game characters.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:19.790
It was among the first, introductory papers
to tackle this problem, and in this series,

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:25.240
we always say that two more papers down the
line, and it will be improved significantly,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:30.730
so let’s see what’s in store, and this
time, just one more paper down the line.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:36.070
This new work offers an impressive value proposition,
which is to transform a real tennis match

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.340
into a realistic looking video game that is
controllable.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.450
This includes synthesizing not only movements,
but also what effect the movements have on

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.960
the ball as well.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.280
So, how do we control this?

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.510
And now, hold on to your papers, because we
can specify where the next shot would land

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.050
with just one click.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.180
For instance, we can place this red dot here.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.690
And now, just think about the fact that this
doesn’t just change where the ball should

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.511
go, but the trajectory of the ball should
be computed using a physical model, and, the

00:01:09.511 --> 00:01:14.220
kind of shot the tennis players has to perform
for the resulting ball trajectory to look

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.220
believable.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:20.690
This physical model even contains the ball’s
spin velocity, and the Magnus effect created

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:21.900
by this spin.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.940
The entire chain of animations has to be correct,
and that’s exactly what happens here.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:27.940
Bravo!

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.170
With blue, we can also specify the position
the player has to await in to hit the ball

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.430
next.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:38.090
And these virtual characters don’t just
look like their real counterparts, they also

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.580
play like them.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:45.220
You see, the authors analyzed the playstyle
of these athletes and built a heatmap that

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.189
contains information about their usual shot
placements for the forehand and backhand shots

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:57.040
separately, the average velocities of these
shots, and even their favored recovery positions.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.189
If you have a closer look at the paper, you
will see that they not only include this kind

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:05.909
of statistical knowledge into their system,
but they really went the extra mile and included

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:08.319
common tennis strategies as well.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:10.009
So, how does it work?

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.959
Let’s look under the hood.

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:16.790
First, it looks at broadcast footage, from
which, annotated clips are extracted that

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.460
contain the movement of these players.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:24.400
If you look carefully, you see this red line
on the spine of the player, and some more,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.709
these are annotations that tell the AI about
the pose of the players.

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:33.000
It builds a database from these clips and
chooses the appropriate piece of footage for

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.311
the action that is about to happen, which
sounds great in theory, but in a moment, you

00:02:38.311 --> 00:02:43.590
will see that this is not nearly enough to
produce a believable animation.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:48.460
For instance, we also need a rendering step,
which has to adjust this footage to the appropriate

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.330
perspective as you see here.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.800
But we have to do way more to make this work.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.800
Look!

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.050
Without additional considerations, we get
something like this.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.050
Not good.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.150
So, what happened here?

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:07.940
Well, given the fact that the source datasets
contain matches that are several hours long,

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.450
they therefore contain many different lighting
conditions.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.590
With this, visual glitches are practically
guaranteed to happen.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:21.360
To address this, the paper describes a normalization
step that can even these changes out.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.360
How well does this do its job?

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.730
Let’s have a look.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:26.780
This is the unnormalized case.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:32.120
This short sequence appears to contain at
least 4 of these glitches, all of which are

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.700
quite apparent.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:38.170
And now, let’s see the new system after
the normalization step.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.520
Yup.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.620
That’s what I am talking about!

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.560
But these are not the only considerations
the authors had to take to produce these amazing

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.560
results.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.819
You see, oftentimes, quite a bit of information
is missing from these frames.

00:03:55.819 --> 00:04:01.340
Our seasoned Fellow Scholars know not to despair,
because we can reach out to image inpainting

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.269
methods to address this.

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:07.620
These can fill in missing details in images
with sensible information.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.410
You can see NVIDIA’s work from two years
ago that could do this reliably for a great

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.470
variety of images.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.299
This new work uses a learning-based technique
called image to image translation to fill

00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:20.969
in these details.

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:27.499
Of course, the advantages of this new system
are visible right away, and so are its limitations.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:32.360
For instance, temporal coherence could be
improved, meaning that the tennis rackets

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.479
can appear or disappear from one frame to
another.

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:41.430
The sprites are not as detailed as they could
be, but, none of this really matters.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:47.050
What matters is that now, what’s been previously
impossible is now possible, and two more papers

00:04:47.050 --> 00:05:12.069
down the line, it is very likely that all
of these issues will be

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:23.849
ironed out.

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:43.149
What 
a time to be alive!

00:05:43.149 --> 00:05:46.649
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

